From: n3ptune <n3ptune@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 14:35:53 
Subject: [PATCH] session: verify power-action exit codes instead of fire-and-forget

Applies to noctalia-shell v5 git, on top of commit 29dab93 (2026-08-29).

requestSuspendDetached()/requestRebootDetached()/requestShutdownDetached()
are the functions the session-panel power button (and IPC) actually call.
They ran in PowerLaunchMode::Detached, which double-fork/execs the
resolved backend (systemctl, loginctl, pm-suspend, zzz, pkexec ...) and
reports success as soon as exec() succeeds -- it never looks at the
command'''s actual exit code. On setups where systemctl does not exist
(e.g. Void/runit) and loginctl is found but fails at the logind/D-Bus
layer (no active seat, elogind not running, polkit denial, etc.),
noctalia considers the action already handled and never tries the next
backend in the list -- the button silently does nothing.

These calls already run off the UI thread (invoke() wraps them in a
detached std::thread via runPowerAction), so there is no need for
process-level fire-and-forget. Switch reboot/shutdown to the
already-existing PowerLaunchMode::Blocking (waits up to
kPowerCommandTimeout and checks the exit code, falling through to the
next backend on failure). Suspend needs its own mode: systemctl/loginctl
suspend legitimately blocks until the system resumes, so the 5s timeout
would kill an in-progress suspend and then race a second backend on
wake -- so a new PowerLaunchMode::BlockingNoTimeout is added and used
for both requestSuspendDetached() and the (currently unused)
suspendBlocking(), which had the same latent bug.

With this, a failing loginctl now logs its actual exit code/stderr and
noctalia automatically falls through to the next configured backend
(pm-suspend, zzz, pkexec ..., or your [shell.session.power] override)
instead of doing nothing.
---
diff --git a/src/shell/session/session_action_runner.cpp b/src/shell/session/session_action_runner.cpp
index ed5ea23..b8c8a0a 100644
--- a/src/shell/session/session_action_runner.cpp
+++ b/src/shell/session/session_action_runner.cpp
@@ -25,7 +25,15 @@ namespace {
   constexpr std::chrono::milliseconds kPowerCommandTimeout{5000};
 
   enum class PowerLaunchMode {
+    // Waits for the command to exit (bounded by kPowerCommandTimeout) so a failure (missing
+    // logind, denied polkit action, etc.) is detected and the next backend is tried.
     Blocking,
+    // Like Blocking, but waits indefinitely instead of applying kPowerCommandTimeout. Needed for
+    // suspend: "systemctl suspend"/"loginctl suspend" block until the system actually resumes, so
+    // a bounded timeout would kill an in-progress suspend and then race a second backend on wake.
+    BlockingNoTimeout,
+    // Fire-and-forget: only confirms the command could be exec'd, never its actual result. Kept
+    // for custom user-configured overrides where we can't reason about the command's semantics.
     Detached,
   };
 
@@ -138,7 +146,9 @@ namespace {
         continue;
       }
 
-      const process::RunResult result = process::runSyncWithTimeout(command, kPowerCommandTimeout);
+      const process::RunResult result = mode == PowerLaunchMode::BlockingNoTimeout
+                                             ? process::runSync(command)
+                                             : process::runSyncWithTimeout(command, kPowerCommandTimeout);
       if (result) {
         kLog.info("{}: {} accepted", action, label);
         cacheIdx = idx;
@@ -298,9 +308,13 @@ bool SessionActionRunner::requestSuspendDetached() const {
     m_hooks.onBeforePlainSuspend();
   }
   std::scoped_lock lock(m_powerMutex);
+  // BlockingNoTimeout: requestSuspendDetached() already runs off the UI thread (invoke() wraps it
+  // in a detached std::thread via runPowerAction), so there is no need for process-level fire-
+  // and-forget here — and verifying the exit code is what lets us fall back to the next backend
+  // when e.g. loginctl execs fine but fails against logind (no active seat, elogind not running).
   const bool started = runPowerActionResolved(
       "suspend", m_suspendCommandOverride, suspendCommandVariants(), m_cachedSuspendAutoStartIdx,
-      PowerLaunchMode::Detached
+      PowerLaunchMode::BlockingNoTimeout
   );
   if (!started && m_hooks.onPlainSuspendAborted) {
     m_hooks.onPlainSuspendAborted();
@@ -311,8 +325,10 @@ bool SessionActionRunner::requestSuspendDetached() const {
 bool SessionActionRunner::requestRebootDetached() const {
   logActionContext("reboot");
   std::scoped_lock lock(m_powerMutex);
+  // See requestSuspendDetached(): already off the UI thread, so verify the exit code (Blocking)
+  // instead of firing the command and assuming success.
   return runPowerActionResolved(
-      "reboot", m_rebootCommandOverride, rebootCommandVariants(), m_cachedRebootAutoStartIdx, PowerLaunchMode::Detached
+      "reboot", m_rebootCommandOverride, rebootCommandVariants(), m_cachedRebootAutoStartIdx, PowerLaunchMode::Blocking
   );
 }
 
@@ -321,7 +337,7 @@ bool SessionActionRunner::requestShutdownDetached() const {
   std::scoped_lock lock(m_powerMutex);
   return runPowerActionResolved(
       "shutdown", m_shutdownCommandOverride, shutdownCommandVariants(), m_cachedShutdownAutoStartIdx,
-      PowerLaunchMode::Detached
+      PowerLaunchMode::Blocking
   );
 }
 
@@ -336,9 +352,11 @@ bool SessionActionRunner::suspendBlocking() const {
     m_hooks.onBeforePlainSuspend();
   }
   std::scoped_lock lock(m_powerMutex);
+  // No timeout: see requestSuspendDetached() — the suspend command legitimately blocks until the
+  // system resumes, so kPowerCommandTimeout must not apply here.
   const bool started = runPowerActionResolved(
       "suspend", m_suspendCommandOverride, suspendCommandVariants(), m_cachedSuspendAutoStartIdx,
-      PowerLaunchMode::Blocking
+      PowerLaunchMode::BlockingNoTimeout
   );
   if (!started && m_hooks.onPlainSuspendAborted) {
     m_hooks.onPlainSuspendAborted();
